---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { site } from "../site";
---

<Base
  title={"About and editorial policy | " + site.name}
  description="AI Guardrails explains how to check model inputs, outputs and actions, with practical examples for people building AI systems."
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">About this site</p>
    <h1>A field guide to AI guardrails.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      AI Guardrails explains how to check model inputs, outputs and actions,
      with practical examples for people building AI systems.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="prose wrap narrow">
    <h2>What you can find here</h2>
    <p>
      A useful guardrail has a specific job: reject an unsafe request, check a
      tool argument, limit access to a resource or catch a bad result. The
      guides explain where each check belongs and how to test the cases it might
      miss.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with{" "}
      <a href="/guides/what-are-ai-guardrails/">what AI guardrails do</a>, then
      work through{" "}
      <a href="/guides/prompt-injection-prevention/">
        prompt injection prevention
      </a>
      , <a href="/guides/tool-calling-guardrails/">tool permissions</a> or{" "}
      <a href="/guides/ai-guardrails-testing/">guardrail testing</a>. The{" "}
      <a href="/tools/policy-checker/">policy lab</a> lets you inspect a small
      decision rule, and the <a href="/resources/">resource library</a> provides
      worksheets and examples to adapt.
    </p>
    <h2>Use the examples in your own system</h2>
    <p>
      Each guide connects a failure to a control and a way to check it. Review
      the assumptions before copying an example: the same read operation may
      expose public status in one system and private records in another.
      Successful tests apply to the configuration and cases they covered.
    </p>
    <h2>Sources and review dates</h2>
    <p>
      Technical claims link to primary sources: standards, project documentation
      or the code that implements a behavior. Each guide has a review date, a
      source list and a downloadable Markdown version. Definitions and examples
      can be cited using the citation at the end of the article.
    </p>
    <p>
      A review date records a content check. Product trials and deployment tests
      need their own evidence. Examples say whether they are conceptual,
      runnable or browser-only demonstrations; fictional records are labeled.
    </p>
    <h2>Product examples and ownership</h2>
    <p>
      A product belongs in a guide when its documented behavior helps explain
      the topic. Comparisons distinguish what the documentation says from what a
      reader still needs to test. Instructions and templates should remain
      useful with other implementations.
    </p>
    <p>
      This publication shares ownership with Emisar, Ryker and co:op. Relevant
      examples disclose that relationship beside the mention.
    </p>
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The interactive tools run in your browser. They do not send the values you
      enter to us. We do not add analytics, advertising scripts or tracking
      cookies. Cloudflare Pages serves the site and may process request data
      under{" "}
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">
        Cloudflare's privacy policy
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
